  "query": "I need a Blackview smartphone, at or under 7999.00 PHP, in stock. There is one firm requirement on the spec that I will tell you if you ask.\n\nTask rules:\nYou are a shopping agent. The user gives firm, non-negotiable requirements: a category, a brand when specified, a specific hardware spec (amount of RAM, screen size, resolution, or refresh rate), a budget, and in-stock availability. EVERY requirement must hold for the EXACT variant you order - a product often sells the right spec and the wrong spec under the same title, so check the specific SKU's option, price, and stock before ordering. Resolution names here denote pixel classes, using the longer and shorter sides regardless of orientation: HD starts at 1280 by 720, Full HD at 1920 by 1080, QHD at 2560 by 1440, and 4K/UHD at 3840 by 2160. Use the highest class whose two thresholds are met; for example, 2560 by 1600 is QHD-class. Lowercase qHD means quarter HD, not QHD. Conflicting resolution classes without a clear selected-variant value are unresolved. Stock can change while you shop; if the variant you wanted sells out, do NOT settle for a different variant of the same product that misses a requirement - find another product that meets all of them. When you need input or want to recommend something, send a short message to the user. Finish by placing the order for a valid, in-stock item that meets every requirement within budget. Once verified, place the order - do not wait for permission or further confirmation. For a smartphone, the title or raw phone/device type must explicitly say smartphone, or its device OS must identify Android, iOS, BlackBerry OS, Windows Phone, Symbian, HarmonyOS, HyperOS, Sailfish, Ubuntu Touch, or Tizen. Category labels alone are insufficient. Reject conflicting tablet, feature/basic-phone, mystery-box, or accessory evidence. View and compare expose raw phone/device type, device OS, and short description type/OS statements, including conflicts. A keypad, QWERTY keyboard, or flip design alone does not disqualify a smartphone. Bundled extras and explicitly negated types are not the device identity; OS compatibility is not an installed OS. Missing positive evidence or marked truncated device facts are unresolved. Check the selected SKU's options: an explicitly screen-only or replacement-screen option is not a complete smartphone, regardless of the parent listing's title.\nThe catalog category_path is available in view and compare. Check that classification against the required category; a phone-like title alone does not establish that a listing is in Smartphones. Also reject accessories and non-device listings when a device is requested.\nRead shopper messages returned after cart actions before placing an order. The shopper may clarify requirements or tighten the budget; once stated, those updates apply to later choices. Answer any direct clarification request.\nUnless the shopper asks for a reason, structured justification is optional. If you supply it, use only field/value claims supported by view or compare of the final product and its current price/stock. Specification claims need raw product facts, not title guesses. Do not include summary or evidence free text.",
